    "answer": "Monitoring maternal weight gain during pregnancy is crucial for several reasons:\n\n**Significance:**\n- Provides indicator of nutritional status and fetal growth\n- Helps identify potential complications early\n- Guides dietary and lifestyle interventions\n\n**Impact on Maternal Health:**\n- Inadequate weight gain increases risk of anemia, preterm delivery, and difficult labor\n- Excessive weight gain increases risk of gestational diabetes, hypertension, preeclampsia, cesarean delivery, and postpartum weight retention\n- Appropriate weight gain supports maternal tissue development (uterus, breasts, blood volume)\n\n**Impact on Fetal Health:**\n- Insufficient gain linked to low birth weight, intrauterine growth restriction, and developmental delays\n- Excessive gain associated with macrosomia (large babies), birth injuries, and childhood obesity risk\n- Adequate nutrition ensures proper organ development and reduces neural tube defects\n\n**Recommended Guidelines:**\n- Weight gain recommendations vary by pre-pregnancy BMI\n- Underweight women: 12.5-18 kg\n- Normal weight: 11.5-16 kg\n- Overweight: 7-11.5 kg\n- Obese: 5-9 kg\n\nRegular monitoring allows healthcare providers to provide personalized guidance for optimal outcomes."
